WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:05.510
Computer-generated effects are ubiquitous
in Hollywood, so when something amazing happens

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:09.639
on screen, audiences usually assume they're
seeing a digital fantasy.

00:00:09.639 --> 00:00:14.360
But despite the surge in CGI, some directors
are still committed to doing special effects

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:17.990
the old-fashioned way, before today's digital
wizardry existed.

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:22.070
From puppets to prosthetics to old-school
camera tricks, we've rounded up some of the

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:26.380
most insane scenes you'll never believe were
created without computers.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:27.380
Flipping out

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:32.720
Fact: director Christopher Nolan actively
dislikes CGI, and believes that audiences

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:37.000
prefer the real thing as much as he does,
so he uses practical effects in his films

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.170
whenever possible.

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:44.180
Remember that epic car chase in The Dark Knight
that ended with a giant 18-wheeler going butt-over-teakettle,

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:47.180
right in the middle of an actual city block?

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:48.880
"He missed!"

00:00:57.130 --> 00:01:01.220
Special effects genies used computers to edit
out some of the equipment involved in the

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:05.150
stunt, but everything else was completely
real, aided by a remote-controlled piston

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:08.550
that was installed in the truck to propel
its rear off the ground.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:12.470
The scene was filmed from seven different
angles, because when you're making an 18-wheeler

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:16.680
do a somersault in the middle of the night
in downtown Chicago, there is no such thing

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:18.240
as "take two."

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:19.860
Anti-gravity fisticuffs

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:23.950
The Dark Knight director didn't limit his
commitment to practical effects only to his

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:28.580
Batman franchise... which, don’t forget,
also included blasting a plane in half, mid-air.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:31.440
"Now what's the next step of your master plan?"

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:33.860
"Crashing this plane!"

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:40.300
In Inception, Nolan and his team created the
gravity-defying dream-within-a-dream fight

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:45.299
scene by channeling none other than talented
singer and hat-enthusiast Jamiroquai and built

00:01:45.299 --> 00:01:48.479
a 100 foot-long hotel hallway on a rotating
set.

00:01:48.479 --> 00:01:53.390
According to actor Joseph Gordon-Levitt, the
filming process required as much mental flexibility

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:56.180
as it did acrobatic balance, telling E! Online,

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:58.659
"I had to think of it like, 'This is the ground.

00:01:58.659 --> 00:02:00.080
OK, now this is the ground.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:01.260
And now this is the ground.'

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.700
It was just that the 'ground' was always moving
under me.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.990
That was the mind game I had to play to make
it work."

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:10.340
With a camera mounted on a track that ran
the length of the set, Nolan was able to create

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:15.049
seamless, stable shots even as the hallway
itself spun 360 degrees.

00:02:15.049 --> 00:02:18.400
But Nolan wasn’t the one who invented the
ol’ rotating room...

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.109
The blood-barfing bed

00:02:20.109 --> 00:02:25.139
CGI wasn't much of an option back in 1984,
especially for a low-budget horror flick like

00:02:25.139 --> 00:02:27.120
Wes Craven's Nightmare on Elm Street.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:32.280
So when Craven's horror franchise introduced
audiences to the original fedora-wearing monster,

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.629
it had to pull off its nightmarish special
effects the old-fashioned way.

00:02:35.629 --> 00:02:39.969
These days, the soul-sucking blood geyser
that consumed Johnny Depp would probably be

00:02:39.969 --> 00:02:44.540
done with computers, but for the original
Nightmare, the effects team came up with an

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:46.629
inventive way to pull it off.

00:02:46.629 --> 00:02:52.580
The bedroom set was built on a rotating axis
which could be turned a full 360 degrees.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:55.159
What you see is actually an upside-down set.

00:02:55.159 --> 00:02:59.590
Once the bedroom was flipped, all they had
to do was pour 500 gallons of fake blood through

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:01.840
the bed, and let gravity do the rest.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.700
Uh, simple, right?

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:04.809
Men becoming wolves

00:03:04.809 --> 00:03:09.860
An American Werewolf in London featured animatronic
heads, prosthetic body parts, and the most

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:13.230
elaborate assortment of merkins in the history
of film.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:17.129
Werewolf’s iconic transformation scene was
achieved with nothing but good old-fashioned

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:19.809
practical effects and the magic of Rick Baker.

00:03:19.809 --> 00:03:24.500
The effects guru created prosthetic versions
of actor David Naughton's limbs, then fitted

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:28.529
them with air bladders to make them deform
as Naughton's character turned from man to

00:03:28.529 --> 00:03:29.529
wolf.

00:03:29.529 --> 00:03:33.459
For the facial transformation, two animatronic
heads created the illusion of the actor's

00:03:33.459 --> 00:03:36.389
head reshaping itself into something more
wolflike.

00:03:36.389 --> 00:03:37.780
And the furry overcoat?

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:42.340
That effect used latex "skin" threaded with
thousands and thousands of wiry hairs.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:46.519
Baker filmed the hair being pulled backwards
through the skin, then reversed the shot to

00:03:46.519 --> 00:03:47.939
simulate rapid growth.

00:03:47.939 --> 00:03:53.219
The final result was so realistic and terrifying
that it earned its creator the 1982 Academy

00:03:53.219 --> 00:03:54.989
Award for Best Makeup.

00:03:54.989 --> 00:03:59.319
And when the movie got its inevitable 21st
century reboot in the form of An American

00:03:59.319 --> 00:04:04.510
Werewolf in Paris, the terrible CGI effects
couldn't hold a candle to this practical wizardry.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:06.169
The killer T-Rex

00:04:06.169 --> 00:04:07.359
Hold onto your butts!

00:04:07.359 --> 00:04:11.480
There's a reason the lizard king of Jurassic
Park was such an imposing presence: he was

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:15.719
a real-life, full-sized animatronic that nearly
killed someone on set.

00:04:15.719 --> 00:04:20.709
The T-Rex weighed 9,000 pounds and was built
on a skeleton made of hydraulic motors and

00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:22.079
moving metal parts.

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:26.250
He was also decidedly dangerous: during the
production, a crew member was unlucky enough

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:30.400
to be stuck inside the belly of the beast
during a power outage, sending thousands of

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:32.800
pounds of hydraulics crushing down on him.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:34.660
Fortunately, he lived to tell the tale.

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:39.470
Even the digital dinosaurs of Jurassic Park
were often a combination of CGI and puppetry.

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:43.600
For instance, the brachiosaurus that sneezed
all over Lex was only a digi-saur from the

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:44.600
neck down.

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:49.159
The head was all real for most of its time
on screen…as was the copious spray of dino

00:04:49.159 --> 00:04:52.560
snot, much to the dismay of actress Ariana
Richards.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:53.910
Little Hobbits, big world

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:59.129
Rather than rely completely on post-production
CGI to shrink Elijah Wood, or embiggen Ian

00:04:59.129 --> 00:05:04.169
McKellen, Peter Jackson often used old-school
optical illusions to make his Hobbits teeny-tiny

00:05:04.169 --> 00:05:08.159
in comparison to all the wizards, orcs, and
elves of Middle-earth.

00:05:08.159 --> 00:05:12.629
Using camera angles, cropping, and the actors'
relative positioning, the effect known as

00:05:12.629 --> 00:05:16.870
"forced perspective" makes them appear to
be differently sized than they actually are.

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:18.120
In theory, it's simple.

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:22.490
In practice, it meant that many shots had
to be staged down to the millimeter throughout

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:27.760
the entire Lord of the Rings trilogy, as well
as The Hobbit trilogy a decade later.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:28.760
Liquid metal evil

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:34.870
In 1991, Terminator 2 was a groundbreaking
example of the power of digital effects, boasting

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:38.039
CGI that went miles beyond anything else that
had been done.

00:05:38.039 --> 00:05:42.750
However, what made the movie so spectacular
wasn't the CGI alone, but the way James Cameron

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:46.939
seamlessly integrated the computer-animated
cuts with practical effects.

00:05:46.939 --> 00:05:52.490
For every purely digital shot of T-1000 slithering
around in liquid metal form, there was a scene

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:57.300
rendered the old-fashioned way, with puppets
and expert camerawork, giving the film a sense

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:58.759
of real-world weight.

00:05:58.759 --> 00:06:03.699
Most incredibly, every time the T-1000 was
blasted apart, Stan Winston's special effects

00:06:03.699 --> 00:06:08.219
team would bring in a mangled, metallic puppet,
complete with a cast of actor Robert Patrick's

00:06:08.219 --> 00:06:09.440
head, to thrash around.

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:14.509
In the T-1000's last moments, the blown-up
puppet is so seamlessly intercut with motion-capture

00:06:14.509 --> 00:06:18.189
effects that you can't even tell where one
ends and the other begins.

00:06:18.189 --> 00:06:19.310
Thanks for watching!

00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:22.130
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:24.389
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

